---
title: Cut-out sugar cookies
servings: 60
yield: 5 dozen 2-inch cookies
tags: [vegetarian, christmas, winter]
cuisine: american
time: 2 hours 42 minutes
source: Holiday Butter Cookies by Susan Reid, King Arthur Baking, https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/holiday-butter-cookies-recipe
---

== Cookies ==

Beat the @confectioners' sugar{142%g}, @salted butter{255%g}(room-temperature),
@egg yolks{1}, @table salt{1/2%tsp} and @vanilla extract{2%tsp} until smooth.
Add the @all-purpose flour{330%g} and mix until smooth. If the dough stays
dry, add a tablespoon of @water{1%tbsp}.

Split it in half, flatten each into a disk, wrap, and chill ~{2%hours} or
overnight.

Heat the oven to 350°F. Let the dough sit out ~{20%minutes} to 30 minutes,
until it rolls but still feels cold.

On a floured counter, roll it 1/8 to 3/16 inch thick and cut shapes with a
#cookie cutter{}. Re-roll the scraps. Set them close together on ungreased or
parchment-lined #baking sheets{}.

Bake ~{12%minutes} to 14 minutes, until set and barely brown at the edges. Cool
on the pan. Repeat with the second disk.

> With unsalted butter, use 1 tsp salt. The source flavours it with 1/4 tsp
> Fiori di Sicilia; 2 tsp vanilla is its suggested swap.

== Icing ==

Stir the @confectioners' sugar{255%g}, @milk{2 1/2%tbsp} and
@light corn syrup{2%tbsp} together. If it is stiff, add more milk a teaspoon at
a time, up to 3 tbsp in all. Tint it with @food colouring{}(optional).

Try it on one cookie and thin or thicken it with milk or sugar as needed.

Ice the cooled cookies a few at a time, scattering @sparkling sugar{1%cup}(optional, or coloured sugar)
over the wet icing. Let it dry to the touch.

> Keeps several days airtight at room temperature; freeze for longer.
